1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,660
Most people believe the Second
World War began on 1 September 1939,

2
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:09,540
when German tanks crossed
the Polish border.

3
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:14,320
Technically, that is true: on that
day the first shots were fired.

4
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:18,740
But the war itself, its
machinery, the anger behind it,

5
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:24,560
the economic desperation that drove it and
the political blindness that made it possible,

6
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:28,020
all of that had been set
in motion decades earlier.

7
00:00:28,420 --> 00:00:30,900
Wars do not come out of nowhere.

8
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:39,540
They are built, piece by piece, decision by
decision, over years and sometimes over generations.

9
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:45,240
The Second World War, the deadliest
conflict in human history, was no exception.

10
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:48,340
It was assembled like a bomb,

11
00:00:48,380 --> 00:00:51,780
component by component,
by politicians, bankers,

12
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:57,360
generals and diplomats who either did
not understand what they were building or

13
00:00:57,400 --> 00:00:59,280
simply did not care.

14
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:07,180
Anyone who really wants to understand why
more than 60 million people died between 1939

15
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:13,800
and 1945 has to turn the clock back
much further than 1939. Back to the

16
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:16,140
end of the First World War.

17
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:23,040
Back to a single document, signed
in a single hall of a single palace.

18
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:28,000
Because the true origin of the
Second World War was written in ink

19
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:30,640
long before it was written in blood.

20
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:37,820
It is 28 June 1919. The place
is the Hall of Mirrors in

21
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:40,280
the Palace of Versailles,
just outside Paris.

22
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:48,480
A vast room, gilded and gleaming, built to
display the power and grandeur of France.

23
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:55,680
On this day it is packed with diplomats, politicians
and officers from all over Europe and beyond.

24
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:58,680
They have come to sign
the Treaty of Versailles,

25
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:04,040
the document meant to officially
end the First World War.

26
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:07,100
At first glance, the treaty
was supposed to bring peace.

27
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:12,780
It was meant to put order into the devastation
left by four years of industrialised killing.

28
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:19,080
The war had cost around 17 million
lives, soldiers and civilians alike.

29
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:23,460
It had wiped out entire
landscapes and toppled empires.

30
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:30,420
Austria-Hungary, the Ottoman Empire, the Russian
Empire and the German Empire had collapsed.

31
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:36,360
Europe lay in ruins, and its statesmen
wanted at any price to prevent

32
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:38,880
anything like it from
ever happening again.

33
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:45,000
But the men who wrote the Treaty of Versailles
were thinking less about a lasting peace.

34
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:47,260
They were thinking about punishment.

35
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:51,500
And the country they wanted to
punish above all was Germany.

36
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:57,560
The treaty contained an article
numbered 231. It would become one of

37
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:00,900
the most consequential
sentences in modern history.

38
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:06,140
In essence, it said that Germany and
its allies bore full responsibility

39
00:03:06,180 --> 00:03:08,560
for the outbreak of the war.

40
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:11,260
This was no diplomatic formality.

41
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:16,820
It was a moral verdict that
declared an entire nation guilty.

42
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:20,020
The German delegation was
not allowed to negotiate.

43
00:03:20,220 --> 00:03:21,720
It was not allowed to change anything.

44
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:23,820
They were given a choice: sign,

45
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:29,840
or the naval blockade continues, the blockade
that was already starving German civilians.

46
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:31,300
They signed.

47
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:36,460
And with that they lit a fuse
that would burn for twenty years.

48
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:41,200
The terms of the treaty went far
beyond an admission of guilt.

49
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:46,460
Germany had to give up around 13
percent of its territory in Europe,

50
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:52,700
more than 70,000 square kilometres, and
with it about a tenth of its population.

51
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:58,220
Between six and seven million people
suddenly lived outside Germany's borders.

52
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:03,680
Alsace-Lorraine returned to France after
almost five decades of German rule.

53
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:08,980
Parts of West Prussia and Upper Silesia
went to the newly founded Polish state.

54
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:15,020
This cut East Prussia off from the rest of the
Reich through the so-called Polish Corridor.

55
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:20,720
The heavily industrialised Saar region with its coal
was placed under League of Nations administration

56
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:24,600
for 15 years, and its
mines went to France.

57
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:29,680
North Schleswig went to Denmark,
Eupen and Malmedy to Belgium.

58
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:34,320
The port city of Danzig, whose
population was almost entirely German,

59
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:37,580
became a Free City under
international supervision.

60
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:41,840
And outside Europe, Germany
lost all of its colonies,

61
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:45,860
territories of almost three million
square kilometres in Africa,

62
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:47,360
Asia and the Pacific.

63
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:53,100
They were divided among the victorious
powers, mainly between Britain and France.

64
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:56,400
But the territorial losses
were only the beginning.

65
00:04:56,980 --> 00:05:01,180
The treaty also imposed drastic
military restrictions on Germany.

66
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:06,300
The once mighty German army was
cut to no more than 100,000 men.

67
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:12,760
The navy was reduced to a remnant fleet,
and an air force was banned altogether.

68
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:17,140
Tanks, heavy artillery and
submarines were forbidden.

69
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:23,680
The Rhineland, the strip of land along the western
border with France, was permanently demilitarised.

70
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:28,960
No German soldiers, no fortifications,
no weapons of any kind.

71
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:32,720
The western flank of the Reich
was left completely exposed.

72
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:35,740
And then came the reparations.

73
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:42,560
In 1921 a reparations commission set the
sum Germany was to pay for war damages:

74
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:49,560
132 billion gold marks, about
33 billion dollars at the time.

75
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:54,860
A sum so enormous that economists
openly said at the time it could not be

76
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:59,040
collected without shaking the
entire world financial system.

77
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:02,640
It was to be paid in gold
and foreign currency,

78
00:06:02,680 --> 00:06:09,320
in raw materials, merchant ships, coal,
livestock and industrial goods, and over decades.

79
00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:12,020
Germany did not just owe money.

80
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:14,820
It owed its economic future.

81
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:18,020
Imagine how this looked from
the German point of view.

82
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:21,000
A brutal war had just ended.

83
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:24,600
Millions of young men were dead or maimed.

84
00:06:24,860 --> 00:06:26,500
The economy was in ruins.

85
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:30,320
The Kaiser had abdicated
and fled to the Netherlands.

86
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:32,660
The country was governed by a young,

87
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:40,780
fragile democracy, the Weimar Republic, which many
Germans had never wanted and even more openly despised.

88
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:44,720
And now they were told the
whole war was Germany's fault.

89
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:50,320
That they should hand over land, colonies,
coal, ships, their army and money.

90
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:57,780
And those who demanded it allowed no negotiation,
no objection, not even a seat at the table.

91
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,040
Sign or starve.

92
00:07:00,540 --> 00:07:04,220
The psychological effect on the
Germans can hardly be overstated.

93
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:07,300
The treaty did not just
weaken the country materially.

94
00:07:07,540 --> 00:07:09,320
It humiliated it to the core.

95
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:15,800
In Germany it was called the Diktat, a
dictated peace rather than a negotiated one.

96
00:07:16,040 --> 00:07:21,020
Many Germans sincerely believed the war
had never been lost on the battlefield.

97
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:25,080
At the armistice, German troops
were still standing on foreign soil.

98
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:32,460
From this grew the so-called stab-in-the-back myth:
the claim that the army had been betrayed from within,

99
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:37,440
by politicians, communists and
others who had given up too early.

100
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:41,900
It was a conspiracy
story, but a powerful one.

101
00:07:41,940 --> 00:07:46,220
And it would be ruthlessly
exploited in the years to come.

102
00:07:46,660 --> 00:07:50,740
The Weimar Republic now found itself
in an almost impossible position.

103
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:56,880
It was born of defeat, tied to
surrender and burdened with an economy

104
00:07:56,920 --> 00:08:02,560
collapsing under reparations,
debt and lost industry.

105
00:08:03,020 --> 00:08:09,300
The government responded to its growing obligations
by printing money, enormous amounts of money.

106
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:14,300
Germany had to pay its reparations
in gold or foreign currency,

107
00:08:14,340 --> 00:08:16,300
but had far too little of either.

108
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:21,400
So the Reichsbank ran the printing
presses at an unprecedented pace,

109
00:08:21,440 --> 00:08:25,480
flooding the economy with
paper marks backed by nothing.

110
00:08:25,940 --> 00:08:28,280
At first the effects remained manageable.

111
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:33,420
Prices rose, but slowly, and
wages more or less kept up.

112
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:36,620
Then everything spun out of control.

113
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:42,780
In January 1923, one US dollar
was worth about 17,000 marks.

114
00:08:42,920 --> 00:08:47,560
That was already a catastrophic
rate, but it would get far worse.

115
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:52,340
In July, a dollar cost
more than 350,000 marks.

116
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:56,020
By the end of October it
was more than 70 billion.

117
00:08:56,320 --> 00:09:04,820
And in November 1923, a single American
dollar was worth 4.2 trillion marks.

118
00:09:04,860 --> 00:09:06,860
4.2 trillion.

119
00:09:07,220 --> 00:09:12,080
In everyday life this meant
workers were paid twice a day,

120
00:09:12,120 --> 00:09:15,800
because their wages lost half their
value between morning and evening.

121
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:20,720
People pushed wheelbarrows full of banknotes
to the baker to buy a loaf of bread.

122
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:26,220
Savings that had taken whole
lifetimes to build vanished overnight.

123
00:09:26,580 --> 00:09:31,940
A pensioner who had carefully saved for
a secure old age suddenly could not buy

124
00:09:31,980 --> 00:09:35,200
even a postage stamp
with his entire fortune.

125
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:41,140
Restaurants stopped printing prices on their
menus because the numbers changed by the hour.

126
00:09:41,380 --> 00:09:44,100
Some shops stopped accepting cash at all.

127
00:09:44,360 --> 00:09:50,460
People burned banknotes for heat, because the
paper was worth more as fuel than as money.

128
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:54,380
The social fabric of
Germany began to come apart.

129
00:09:54,660 --> 00:10:00,000
The middle class, the backbone of every
stable democracy, was practically wiped out.

130
00:10:00,040 --> 00:10:07,240
Academics, teachers, civil servants, small business
owners, all those who had followed the rules,

131
00:10:07,280 --> 00:10:12,240
saved and trusted their institutions,
were now left with nothing.

132
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:15,040
And this is the crucial point:

133
00:10:15,080 --> 00:10:19,180
it was not the very poor or
the very rich who radicalised.

134
00:10:19,420 --> 00:10:21,080
It was the middle class.

135
00:10:21,380 --> 00:10:25,400
People who had something to
lose, then lost everything,

136
00:10:25,440 --> 00:10:30,960
and who were now desperately looking for
someone to blame and someone to follow.

137
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:35,940
It was at exactly this
time, in November 1923,

138
00:10:35,980 --> 00:10:42,700
that a then little-known political agitator
named Adolf Hitler made his first grab for power.

139
00:10:43,260 --> 00:10:48,140
With a few hundred followers he
stormed a beer hall in Munich to launch

140
00:10:48,180 --> 00:10:50,640
a coup against the
Bavarian state government.

141
00:10:51,260 --> 00:10:56,500
The attempt was amateurish, chaotic
and collapsed within a few hours.

142
00:10:56,960 --> 00:11:02,620
Hitler was arrested, tried and sentenced
to five years of fortress confinement,

143
00:11:02,660 --> 00:11:05,440
of which he served less than nine months.

144
00:11:06,060 --> 00:11:09,620
In prison at Landsberg he dictated a
book setting out his political ideology,

145
00:11:09,660 --> 00:11:14,060
his deep resentment of
the Treaty of Versailles,

146
00:11:14,100 --> 00:11:20,080
his conspiracy theories about who was
to blame for Germany's humiliation,

147
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:23,660
and his apocalyptic vision of the future.

148
00:11:24,340 --> 00:11:26,980
The book was called Mein Kampf.

149
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:31,420
The blueprint for everything that
followed was laid out in it for

150
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:33,340
anyone who cared to read it.

151
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:36,140
But at the time hardly anyone did.

152
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:40,280
At this point everything could
still have turned out differently.

153
00:11:41,400 --> 00:11:48,740
In 1924 hyperinflation was brought under control with
the introduction of a new currency, the Rentenmark.

154
00:11:49,130 --> 00:11:52,720
In the same year the Dawes
Plan came into force,

155
00:11:52,760 --> 00:11:55,660
named after the American
banker Charles Dawes,

156
00:11:55,700 --> 00:11:57,380
who had chaired the committee.

157
00:11:57,800 --> 00:12:03,660
The plan was meant to stabilise the German
economy and get reparation payments moving again.

158
00:12:04,340 --> 00:12:08,500
It spread Germany's payments over
more manageable annual instalments.

159
00:12:08,820 --> 00:12:13,900
Above all, though, it opened the
floodgates to huge American loans.

160
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:20,760
Over the following years, American banks and
financial houses lent Germany billions of dollars.

161
00:12:21,340 --> 00:12:22,960
Wall Street firms such as J.P.

162
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:30,720
Morgan, Dillon, Read and the National City Bank
issued huge bonds to finance German industry.

163
00:12:31,060 --> 00:12:35,160
The money flowed into chemical
groups like IG Farben,

164
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:39,980
steel giants like Vereinigte
Stahlwerke and many other companies.

165
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:43,520
At first glance this looked like a rescue.

166
00:12:43,560 --> 00:12:46,840
And in many ways it worked.

167
00:12:47,260 --> 00:12:55,480
The economy stabilised, industrial output rose,
unemployment fell and cultural life flourished.

168
00:12:56,160 --> 00:13:04,400
The years from about 1924 to 1929 went
down in history as the Golden Twenties.

169
00:13:05,060 --> 00:13:08,200
Berlin became one of the
liveliest cities in Europe,

170
00:13:08,240 --> 00:13:13,280
a centre of art, film,
music and intellectual life.

171
00:13:13,880 --> 00:13:17,400
The Weimar Republic finally
seemed to find its footing.

172
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:22,400
Democracy, it seemed, might
really take root in Germany.

173
00:13:22,680 --> 00:13:28,800
But inside this apparent boom lay a
fatal flaw, and it was structural.

174
00:13:29,180 --> 00:13:34,400
Germany's recovery depended almost entirely
on a constant inflow of American capital.

175
00:13:34,440 --> 00:13:39,900
The loans did not build an economy
that could stand on its own.

176
00:13:40,200 --> 00:13:42,220
They built a house of cards.

177
00:13:42,560 --> 00:13:46,700
German companies and municipalities
borrowed large sums from American banks,

178
00:13:46,740 --> 00:13:53,680
and part of that borrowed money went into
reparation payments to France and Britain.

179
00:13:53,840 --> 00:13:59,460
France and Britain in turn used
those reparations to repay their own

180
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:01,120
war debts to the United States.

181
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:04,020
So the money went round in a circle.

182
00:14:04,160 --> 00:14:10,180
American banks lent money to Germany,
Germany paid France and Britain,

183
00:14:10,220 --> 00:14:14,960
and France and Britain paid the
money back to the American banks.

184
00:14:15,200 --> 00:14:17,420
And the cycle started all over again.

185
00:14:17,740 --> 00:14:23,360
Everyone was borrowing, and no one was building
anything that could survive without new credit.

186
00:14:23,660 --> 00:14:29,580
If American capital ever dried up,
the whole structure would collapse.

187
00:14:30,580 --> 00:14:35,020
In 1929 it did collapse,
and spectacularly.

188
00:14:35,140 --> 00:14:40,980
On 24 October 1929 a crash began
on the New York Stock Exchange that

189
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:44,580
wiped out billions of dollars within days.

190
00:14:44,780 --> 00:14:48,200
It set off a chain
reaction of bank failures,

191
00:14:48,240 --> 00:14:53,080
business closures and economic collapse
that went down in history as the Great

192
00:14:53,120 --> 00:14:56,500
Depression, the worst economic
crisis of the modern era.

193
00:14:56,540 --> 00:15:00,720
In the United States the
consequences were devastating.

194
00:15:00,880 --> 00:15:09,620
By 1933 around 15 million Americans were
unemployed, more than 20 percent of the workforce.

195
00:15:09,720 --> 00:15:12,020
But Germany was hit even harder.

196
00:15:12,420 --> 00:15:19,180
American banks urgently needed money and immediately
called in their short-term loans from Europe.

197
00:15:19,620 --> 00:15:26,200
For Germany, which had become completely dependent
on those loans, it was the economic death blow.

198
00:15:26,480 --> 00:15:29,520
The golden years vanished
almost overnight.

199
00:15:29,900 --> 00:15:34,700
Banks collapsed, factories
closed, businesses went bankrupt.

200
00:15:34,980 --> 00:15:40,580
At the end of 1929 about 1.5
million Germans were unemployed.

201
00:15:40,900 --> 00:15:46,760
By the end of 1930 the number had more
than doubled to almost four million.

202
00:15:46,980 --> 00:15:53,800
And by early 1933 it was six million,
around 30 percent of the workforce.

203
00:15:54,040 --> 00:15:57,320
In some industrial regions
the rate was even higher.

204
00:15:57,800 --> 00:15:59,900
Families were thrown out of their homes.

205
00:15:59,940 --> 00:16:04,340
The queues outside the soup kitchens
stretched along entire streets.

206
00:16:04,500 --> 00:16:06,660
The number of suicides rose.

207
00:16:06,880 --> 00:16:09,720
Despair was everywhere.

208
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:13,100
And once again the middle
class was hit hardest.

209
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:18,460
The same people who had been ruined by
hyperinflation only a few years earlier,

210
00:16:18,500 --> 00:16:22,840
and had only just recovered,
were now ruined a second time.

211
00:16:23,240 --> 00:16:25,720
This time many of them had had enough.

212
00:16:26,120 --> 00:16:32,940
They had lost faith in the Weimar Republic,
in capitalism and in democracy itself.

213
00:16:33,220 --> 00:16:39,920
And they were ready to follow anyone who
promised order, dignity and national strength.

214
00:16:40,200 --> 00:16:43,460
This was the moment Adolf
Hitler had been waiting for.

215
00:16:43,920 --> 00:16:48,500
His NSDAP had been a fringe
party during the Golden Twenties.

216
00:16:48,820 --> 00:16:51,920
Now it suddenly found a huge audience.

217
00:16:52,160 --> 00:16:58,560
In the 1928 Reichstag election it
had won just 2.6 percent of the vote.

218
00:16:58,600 --> 00:17:02,780
But in September 1930, at the first
election after the crisis began,

219
00:17:02,820 --> 00:17:06,680
that figure shot up to 18.3 percent.

220
00:17:07,040 --> 00:17:10,900
The NSDAP was now the second
largest party in Germany.

221
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:16,460
By July 1932, with 37
percent, it was the largest.

222
00:17:16,640 --> 00:17:22,720
And on 30 January 1933 Adolf Hitler
was appointed Reich Chancellor.

223
00:17:23,220 --> 00:17:26,880
In economic terms, something
remarkable had happened here.

224
00:17:26,920 --> 00:17:33,040
The same financial system that was meant
to stabilise Germany through the Dawes Plan

225
00:17:33,080 --> 00:17:35,940
had unintentionally created the
conditions for its destruction.

226
00:17:36,180 --> 00:17:44,700
American capital had flowed into Germany, created
artificial prosperity, built dependence and then vanished.

227
00:17:44,820 --> 00:17:53,040
What remained was an economic wasteland, fertile ground for
the most destructive political movement of modern history.

228
00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:54,800
This was not a conspiracy.

229
00:17:54,840 --> 00:17:58,780
It was negligence on a historic scale.

230
00:17:59,360 --> 00:18:04,320
But it was not only in Germany that the
interwar years were preparing future conflicts.

231
00:18:04,620 --> 00:18:09,060
To understand the full scale of
what led to the Second World War,

232
00:18:09,100 --> 00:18:11,560
you have to look east, to Japan.

233
00:18:12,300 --> 00:18:18,340
In the 1920s and 1930s Japan
was a country in upheaval.

234
00:18:18,600 --> 00:18:22,980
In the preceding decades it had industrialised
rapidly and risen to become one of

235
00:18:23,020 --> 00:18:26,840
the strongest military and
economic powers in Asia.

236
00:18:27,300 --> 00:18:32,360
In the First World War Japan had fought
on the side of the Allies and expected to

237
00:18:32,400 --> 00:18:36,240
be treated as an equal
partner in the postwar order.

238
00:18:36,620 --> 00:18:39,440
Instead it felt deeply slighted.

239
00:18:39,760 --> 00:18:45,760
At the Paris Peace Conference the Western powers
rejected Japan's proposal to include a racial equality

240
00:18:45,800 --> 00:18:48,800
clause in the Covenant
of the League of Nations.

241
00:18:49,160 --> 00:18:53,720
The message was clear: as a
military ally Japan was useful,

242
00:18:53,760 --> 00:19:00,220
but as a true equal it was not recognised
by the Western colonial powers.

243
00:19:01,180 --> 00:19:05,580
The Great Depression hit Japan
hard, especially in the countryside.

244
00:19:05,900 --> 00:19:13,600
Silk exports, a vital source of income for Japanese
farmers, collapsed when American demand disappeared.

245
00:19:13,940 --> 00:19:18,660
Millions of farming families fell
into poverty, and food grew scarce.

246
00:19:18,700 --> 00:19:25,220
The political leadership, seen as corrupt
and incompetent, increasingly lost control.

247
00:19:25,840 --> 00:19:29,240
Into this vacuum stepped
the Japanese military,

248
00:19:29,280 --> 00:19:34,340
which had grown ever more powerful
and nationalistic during the 1920s.

249
00:19:34,440 --> 00:19:42,060
On 18 September 1931, officers of Japan's
Kwantung Army staged a false flag incident

250
00:19:42,100 --> 00:19:46,180
near the city of Mukden
in Chinese Manchuria.

251
00:19:46,660 --> 00:19:52,500
They set off a small explosive charge
on the South Manchuria Railway,

252
00:19:52,540 --> 00:19:57,280
blamed Chinese saboteurs and used it
as a pretext for a full-scale invasion.

253
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:06,600
Within a few months the Japanese army conquered
the entire region and set up a puppet state called

254
00:20:06,640 --> 00:20:11,440
Manchukuo, with an emperor as
figurehead, fully controlled from Tokyo.

255
00:20:11,820 --> 00:20:16,380
This was an open attack on another
member of the League of Nations.

256
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:19,180
China asked the League
of Nations for help.

257
00:20:19,740 --> 00:20:25,960
The League sent a commission of inquiry
led by the British diplomat Lord Lytton.

258
00:20:26,380 --> 00:20:29,880
The commission took more than
a year to produce its report.

259
00:20:30,180 --> 00:20:35,700
It stated that Japan's actions were not
justified and that Chinese sovereignty

260
00:20:35,740 --> 00:20:38,680
over Manchuria had to be restored.

261
00:20:39,340 --> 00:20:47,060
Japan's answer was simple and devastating: it left
the League of Nations and stayed in Manchuria.

262
00:20:47,500 --> 00:20:49,780
And this is the decisive point.

263
00:20:50,280 --> 00:20:54,940
The League of Nations did
absolutely nothing to stop Japan.

264
00:20:55,400 --> 00:20:59,360
It had no army and no means
of enforcing its decisions.

265
00:20:59,660 --> 00:21:04,780
The United States, the most powerful
country in the world, was not even a member.

266
00:21:05,200 --> 00:21:09,240
Britain and France, the two
most influential members,

267
00:21:09,280 --> 00:21:13,320
were caught up in their own economic
crises and had no interest in

268
00:21:13,360 --> 00:21:16,780
confronting Japan on the
other side of the world.

269
00:21:17,160 --> 00:21:24,040
So the League published its report, Japan
ignored it, and the world carried on as usual.

270
00:21:24,080 --> 00:21:28,620
This was more than a failure
of international diplomacy.

271
00:21:29,080 --> 00:21:35,360
It was a signal, loud and clear, to
every expansionist power in the world:

272
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:41,540
the international system meant to prevent
wars was in truth completely toothless.

273
00:21:42,180 --> 00:21:45,160
If Japan could invade a sovereign state,

274
00:21:45,200 --> 00:21:49,580
defy the League of Nations and
suffer no consequences at all,

275
00:21:49,620 --> 00:21:50,880
then others could too.

276
00:21:50,920 --> 00:21:55,780
In Italy, Benito Mussolini
was watching very closely.

277
00:21:56,320 --> 00:22:03,220
Mussolini had ruled Italy as a fascist
dictator since 1922 and dreamed of

278
00:22:03,260 --> 00:22:06,220
restoring the glory of the Roman Empire.

279
00:22:06,780 --> 00:22:11,920
His eye fell on Ethiopia, one of the
last independent states in Africa.

280
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:19,460
Of all places, it was Ethiopia that had
defeated an Italian invasion army in

281
00:22:19,500 --> 00:22:22,220
1896, a humiliation that
still rankled in Italy.

282
00:22:22,700 --> 00:22:26,980
In October 1935 Mussolini
launched his attack.

283
00:22:27,420 --> 00:22:32,400
The Ethiopian emperor Haile Selassie desperately
asked the League of Nations for help.

284
00:22:32,680 --> 00:22:38,500
He travelled to Geneva in person and gave one of
the most moving speeches in diplomatic history,

285
00:22:38,540 --> 00:22:43,820
imploring the assembled delegates to honour
their promise of collective security.

286
00:22:44,220 --> 00:22:50,120
The League imposed economic sanctions on
Italy, but they were deliberately watered down.

287
00:22:50,360 --> 00:22:57,540
Oil, coal and steel, exactly what Italy
needed for its war, were expressly excluded.

288
00:22:58,040 --> 00:23:03,580
Britain and France feared driving Mussolini
into Hitler's arms and secretly negotiated the

289
00:23:03,620 --> 00:23:10,200
Hoare-Laval Pact, a backroom deal that
would have handed Italy two thirds of

290
00:23:10,240 --> 00:23:12,700
Ethiopia in exchange for peace.

291
00:23:12,980 --> 00:23:16,580
When the plan leaked to the
press, there was a scandal.

292
00:23:17,060 --> 00:23:25,120
But it revealed the uncomfortable truth: the Western democracies
cared more about keeping good relations with dictators

293
00:23:25,160 --> 00:23:28,460
than about defending the principles
they claimed to stand for.

294
00:23:28,960 --> 00:23:33,560
In May 1936 Italian
troops took Addis Ababa.

295
00:23:33,600 --> 00:23:36,780
Ethiopia was completely conquered.

296
00:23:37,300 --> 00:23:40,060
The League of Nations had failed again,

297
00:23:40,100 --> 00:23:42,860
and this time the failure weighed
even heavier than in Manchuria,

298
00:23:42,900 --> 00:23:48,260
because it happened before the eyes of Europe
and not on the other side of the world.

299
00:23:48,620 --> 00:23:53,040
Every dictator on the continent
could see that the system meant to

300
00:23:53,080 --> 00:23:55,720
prevent aggression was not only weak.

301
00:23:55,940 --> 00:24:00,940
It was even willing to sacrifice
smaller states to appease larger ones.

302
00:24:01,040 --> 00:24:07,960
Meanwhile in Germany, Hitler followed all of this with
the attention of a predator studying its territory.

303
00:24:08,380 --> 00:24:12,280
And step by step he tore the
Treaty of Versailles apart.

304
00:24:12,820 --> 00:24:17,820
As early as October 1933 he had taken
Germany out of the League of Nations.

305
00:24:18,800 --> 00:24:24,360
In 1935 he openly announced the reintroduction
of conscription and the building of a new German

306
00:24:24,400 --> 00:24:28,880
air force, both a direct
breach of the treaty.

307
00:24:29,280 --> 00:24:34,300
The reaction from London and
Paris was strikingly restrained.

308
00:24:34,780 --> 00:24:40,520
In June 1935 Britain even concluded
the Anglo-German Naval Agreement,

309
00:24:40,560 --> 00:24:45,620
which allowed Germany a navy of up
to 35 percent of the Royal Navy.

310
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:50,840
The agreement was made without consulting
France and effectively legitimised the violation

311
00:24:50,880 --> 00:24:54,360
of the military terms of Versailles.

312
00:24:55,100 --> 00:25:00,860
For Hitler the message was unmistakable: the
Western powers were willing to negotiate away

313
00:25:00,900 --> 00:25:04,220
the treaty's restrictions
rather than enforce them.

314
00:25:04,960 --> 00:25:11,780
Then came 7 March 1936. On that day
Hitler ordered German troops into

315
00:25:11,820 --> 00:25:17,880
the Rhineland, the demilitarised zone
the Treaty of Versailles had created as a

316
00:25:17,920 --> 00:25:20,160
buffer between Germany and France.

317
00:25:20,760 --> 00:25:22,920
It was an enormous gamble.

318
00:25:23,340 --> 00:25:28,100
At that point the Wehrmacht was still
relatively small and poorly equipped.

319
00:25:28,500 --> 00:25:33,120
Hitler himself later admitted that
the 48 hours after the march in

320
00:25:33,160 --> 00:25:36,480
were the most nerve-racking of his life.

321
00:25:37,100 --> 00:25:44,080
His commanders had orders to withdraw immediately
if the French army responded militarily.

322
00:25:44,540 --> 00:25:46,760
But France did nothing.

323
00:25:47,420 --> 00:25:50,020
The French government was paralysed.

324
00:25:50,060 --> 00:25:56,140
It consulted Britain, and London
advised against any military response.

325
00:25:56,680 --> 00:26:02,820
The British view, in essence, was that Germany
was only walking into its own back garden.

326
00:26:03,320 --> 00:26:05,520
And so the moment passed.

327
00:26:06,160 --> 00:26:07,820
Hitler's gamble paid off.

328
00:26:08,400 --> 00:26:12,080
He had taken back the Rhineland
without firing a shot,

329
00:26:12,120 --> 00:26:15,380
and he had proven something
far more important:

330
00:26:15,420 --> 00:26:20,320
the Western democracies would not
fight to defend the postwar order.

331
00:26:20,700 --> 00:26:27,000
They would protest, condemn and wring
their hands, but they would not act.

332
00:26:27,620 --> 00:26:30,380
This emboldened Hitler beyond measure.

333
00:26:30,760 --> 00:26:35,300
Every provocation that went
unanswered made the next one easier.

334
00:26:35,760 --> 00:26:41,240
Every line crossed without consequences
made the next one seem less important.

335
00:26:41,280 --> 00:26:45,280
In this slow game of
testing and escalating,

336
00:26:45,320 --> 00:26:50,620
Hitler refined the strategy that
would carry him to the brink of war.

337
00:26:51,300 --> 00:26:54,140
At the same time another
conflict was unfolding,

338
00:26:54,180 --> 00:26:58,240
one sometimes called the dress
rehearsal for the Second World War.

339
00:26:58,880 --> 00:27:06,120
In July 1936 a group of officers that
included General Francisco Franco tried

340
00:27:06,160 --> 00:27:10,620
to overthrow Spain's democratically
elected Republican government.

341
00:27:10,940 --> 00:27:18,180
The country split in two, and a brutal civil
war began that would last almost three years.

342
00:27:18,740 --> 00:27:25,300
What made the Spanish Civil War so significant
on the road to world war was foreign involvement.

343
00:27:25,940 --> 00:27:34,260
Hitler and Mussolini immediately backed Franco's
Nationalists, sending troops, aircraft, tanks and ammunition.

344
00:27:34,740 --> 00:27:38,320
Germany's Condor Legion gained
valuable combat experience in Spain,

345
00:27:38,360 --> 00:27:47,300
testing tactics, weapons and methods that would be
used on a far larger scale only a few years later.

346
00:27:47,940 --> 00:27:54,860
The destruction of the Basque town of Guernica by
German and Italian bombers in April 1937 became

347
00:27:54,900 --> 00:27:58,640
one of the most harrowing
episodes of the war.

348
00:27:58,980 --> 00:28:05,480
A deliberate attack on civilians that foreshadowed
the bombing campaigns of the coming world war.

349
00:28:05,580 --> 00:28:12,120
The Western democracies meanwhile relied on non-intervention
and officially supplied weapons to neither side.

350
00:28:12,560 --> 00:28:14,960
This policy was a fig leaf.

351
00:28:15,360 --> 00:28:21,700
Germany and Italy supplied Franco quite
openly while Britain and France looked on.

352
00:28:22,020 --> 00:28:28,300
The Soviet Union did support the Republic,
but irregularly and not without conditions.

353
00:28:28,700 --> 00:28:35,860
The Spanish Republic was abandoned by exactly the
democracies that should have been its natural allies.

354
00:28:36,240 --> 00:28:38,900
Spain became the laboratory of modern war,

355
00:28:38,940 --> 00:28:46,000
the testing ground for the weapons, tactics and
alliances that would shape the Second World War.

356
00:28:46,440 --> 00:28:53,040
And it confirmed the pattern already seen
in Manchuria, Ethiopia and the Rhineland.

357
00:28:53,360 --> 00:28:55,380
Aggression was rewarded.

358
00:28:55,660 --> 00:28:57,640
Inaction was the rule.

359
00:28:58,040 --> 00:29:03,940
And the institutions meant to prevent
wars would not or could not act.

360
00:29:04,900 --> 00:29:11,340
By 1938 the pattern was set, and Hitler
was ready for his boldest move yet.

361
00:29:11,800 --> 00:29:15,040
In March German troops
marched into Austria.

362
00:29:15,440 --> 00:29:20,520
The so-called Anschluss made Austria
part of the Greater German Reich.

363
00:29:20,760 --> 00:29:25,020
Once again the Western powers protested,
and once again they did not act.

364
00:29:25,340 --> 00:29:31,000
Austria, a German-speaking country with
a strong National Socialist following,

365
00:29:31,040 --> 00:29:35,100
was absorbed into the Third
Reich without a single shot.

366
00:29:35,600 --> 00:29:37,740
Then came the Sudeten crisis.

367
00:29:38,200 --> 00:29:46,540
The Sudetenland was a mountainous, industrialised region in
western Czechoslovakia where more than three million Germans lived.

368
00:29:46,940 --> 00:29:54,420
Hitler demanded that the territory be handed to Germany, citing
the alleged oppression of the German-speaking population.

369
00:29:54,700 --> 00:29:59,940
Czechoslovakia was a democracy,
well armed and ready to fight.

370
00:30:00,120 --> 00:30:05,140
It had a modern army, strong border
fortifications and mutual assistance treaties

371
00:30:05,180 --> 00:30:07,320
with France and the Soviet Union.

372
00:30:07,760 --> 00:30:11,260
But it would never get the
chance to defend itself.

373
00:30:11,980 --> 00:30:20,080
In September 1938 British Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain
flew to Munich to negotiate directly with Hitler.

374
00:30:20,400 --> 00:30:27,260
With him came French Prime Minister Édouard
Daladier and the Italian dictator Benito Mussolini.

375
00:30:27,300 --> 00:30:30,720
Czechoslovakia was not invited.

376
00:30:31,320 --> 00:30:34,560
On 30 September the four
powers reached agreement.

377
00:30:34,820 --> 00:30:37,920
Germany was allowed to occupy
the Sudetenland immediately.

378
00:30:38,280 --> 00:30:41,120
Czechoslovakia's objections
were brushed aside.

379
00:30:41,220 --> 00:30:46,900
Its border fortifications, its industry and
its strategic position in Central Europe

380
00:30:46,940 --> 00:30:49,420
were sacrificed on the
altar of appeasement.

381
00:30:49,940 --> 00:30:54,980
Chamberlain flew back to London, waved
a sheet of paper and announced that

382
00:30:55,020 --> 00:30:57,600
he had achieved peace for our time.

383
00:30:58,080 --> 00:31:03,640
The crowd cheered him, desperate to
believe that war had been averted.

384
00:31:04,120 --> 00:31:06,360
But not everyone was convinced.

385
00:31:06,620 --> 00:31:12,320
Winston Churchill, then an ordinary member
of parliament, delivered a damning verdict.

386
00:31:12,820 --> 00:31:16,960
Britain and France had been given
the choice between dishonour and war.

387
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:21,940
They had chosen dishonour,
and they would get war anyway.

388
00:31:22,700 --> 00:31:24,100
Churchill was proved right.

389
00:31:24,500 --> 00:31:28,420
Only six months later, in March 1939,

390
00:31:28,460 --> 00:31:33,640
German troops marched into what remained of
Czechoslovakia and occupied the whole country.

391
00:31:34,080 --> 00:31:39,440
The Munich Agreement, meant to secure peace,
was waste paper before the year was out.

392
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:42,620
Hitler had never intended to keep it.

393
00:31:42,880 --> 00:31:48,900
It had only been a tool to eliminate
Czechoslovakia without having to fight for it.

394
00:31:49,560 --> 00:31:53,540
At this point something changed in the
calculations of the Western powers.

395
00:31:53,880 --> 00:31:57,860
The occupation of Czechoslovakia was
different from everything before.

396
00:31:58,160 --> 00:32:02,760
The Rhineland, Austria and even
the Sudetenland could be presented,

397
00:32:02,800 --> 00:32:08,820
however thinly, as the recovery of territories
that were ethnically or historically German.

398
00:32:09,220 --> 00:32:14,200
The occupation of Czechoslovakia, by contrast,
was the conquest of a foreign people.

399
00:32:14,660 --> 00:32:20,380
It was naked imperialism, and it finally
destroyed the illusion that Hitler's

400
00:32:20,420 --> 00:32:23,040
ambition could be satisfied
through negotiation.

401
00:32:23,620 --> 00:32:31,680
Britain and France gave guarantees to Poland, promising
to defend its independence if it were attacked.

402
00:32:31,780 --> 00:32:36,900
But by then the strategic situation
had already deteriorated dramatically.

403
00:32:37,080 --> 00:32:42,820
The Rhineland was remilitarised, Austria
annexed, Czechoslovakia broken up.

404
00:32:43,360 --> 00:32:45,800
The League of Nations was an empty shell.

405
00:32:46,180 --> 00:32:52,240
And for almost a decade the Western
democracies had shown that they would

406
00:32:52,280 --> 00:32:54,700
sacrifice smaller states
to avoid a confrontation.

407
00:32:55,220 --> 00:32:58,000
Then came the last piece of the puzzle,

408
00:32:58,040 --> 00:33:03,980
the event that made the attack on
Poland not just likely but inevitable.

409
00:33:04,480 --> 00:33:10,340
On 23 August 1939 Germany and the
Soviet Union shocked the world with

410
00:33:10,380 --> 00:33:12,960
a non-aggression pact,
the Hitler-Stalin Pact,

411
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:19,120
also called the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact
after the two countries' foreign ministers.

412
00:33:19,580 --> 00:33:23,720
Officially it was a simple
agreement not to attack each other.

413
00:33:23,760 --> 00:33:30,820
But a secret additional protocol divided Eastern
Europe into a German and a Soviet sphere of interest.

414
00:33:31,140 --> 00:33:33,860
Poland was to be split between them.

415
00:33:34,400 --> 00:33:38,720
With the Soviet Union removed as
a threat, Hitler had a free hand.

416
00:33:39,200 --> 00:33:45,380
Nine days later, on 1 September
1939, the Wehrmacht invaded Poland.

417
00:33:45,580 --> 00:33:50,020
Two days after that, Britain and
France declared war on Germany.

418
00:33:50,060 --> 00:33:53,720
The Second World War had officially begun.

419
00:33:54,280 --> 00:34:02,140
But as we have seen, the war did not begin
on 1 September 1939. That day merely saw

420
00:34:02,180 --> 00:34:07,440
the explosion of twenty years of
failure, resentment and misjudgement.

421
00:34:07,840 --> 00:34:13,080
The true origins of the Second World War
reach back through the entire interwar period.

422
00:34:13,500 --> 00:34:17,360
Through the appeasement of dictators,
through the collapse of collective security,

423
00:34:17,400 --> 00:34:22,620
through the Great Depression and
the artificial prosperity before it,

424
00:34:22,660 --> 00:34:26,140
through the hyperinflation that
destroyed a whole generation's savings,

425
00:34:26,180 --> 00:34:33,600
through the harsh reparations that crippled Germany's
economy, and all the way back to a single document

426
00:34:33,640 --> 00:34:37,620
signed in a gilded hall at Versailles.

427
00:34:38,300 --> 00:34:41,600
And that is exactly what makes
this story so disturbing:

428
00:34:41,640 --> 00:34:45,720
at almost every stage the
catastrophe could have been avoided.

429
00:34:46,070 --> 00:34:53,180
Had the Treaty of Versailles been less harsh, Germany
might not have been driven into such resentment.

430
00:34:53,480 --> 00:35:00,500
Had the Dawes Plan built a truly independent economy
instead of creating dependence on foreign loans,

431
00:35:00,540 --> 00:35:04,560
the Great Depression might not
have hit Germany so devastatingly.

432
00:35:04,700 --> 00:35:08,400
Had the League of Nations
stood up to Japan in Manchuria,

433
00:35:08,440 --> 00:35:11,960
Mussolini might have
hesitated to attack Ethiopia.

434
00:35:12,240 --> 00:35:16,360
Had France marched into
the Rhineland in 1936,

435
00:35:16,400 --> 00:35:18,860
when it was militarily far superior,

436
00:35:18,900 --> 00:35:21,400
Hitler's career might have ended there.

437
00:35:21,600 --> 00:35:27,460
And had Britain and France stood by
Czechoslovakia in 1938 instead of giving it up

438
00:35:27,500 --> 00:35:31,480
at Munich, the war might
never have broken out.

439
00:35:31,840 --> 00:35:33,540
But they did not.

440
00:35:33,740 --> 00:35:40,720
At every turning point, those who could have prevented
the catastrophe chose the path of least resistance.

441
00:35:40,760 --> 00:35:44,880
They chose short-term calm
over long-term security.

442
00:35:45,260 --> 00:35:48,040
They chose appeasement over confrontation.

443
00:35:48,340 --> 00:35:52,380
They chose the illusion of
peace over real deterrence.

444
00:35:52,520 --> 00:35:56,900
And in doing so they made possible the
greatest catastrophe in human history.

445
00:35:57,000 --> 00:36:04,620
There is a deeper lesson in this story, one
that reaches far beyond the 1920s and 1930s.

446
00:36:04,880 --> 00:36:08,080
It is about the interplay
between economics,

447
00:36:08,120 --> 00:36:13,160
politics and the conditions under which
societies become vulnerable to extremism.

448
00:36:13,640 --> 00:36:17,740
Many authoritarian movements in history
have followed a similar pattern.

449
00:36:18,140 --> 00:36:22,620
First comes the economic crisis,
then national humiliation,

450
00:36:22,660 --> 00:36:30,300
then the search for scapegoats, and finally the
strongman who promises to restore lost greatness.

451
00:36:30,780 --> 00:36:34,700
In Germany this pattern played
out with devastating precision,

452
00:36:34,740 --> 00:36:39,580
but to varying degrees it also
appeared in Italy, Japan and Spain.

453
00:36:39,940 --> 00:36:47,000
Economic despair, combined with a sense of
national humiliation and a loss of faith in

454
00:36:47,040 --> 00:36:53,360
democratic institutions, created the conditions in
which authoritarian leaders could come to power and lead

455
00:36:53,400 --> 00:36:54,880
their countries into war.

456
00:36:55,360 --> 00:37:00,680
And the international financial system did
not prevent this; it often made it worse.

457
00:37:01,280 --> 00:37:09,660
The cycle of loans, reparations and debt repayments
created a fragile web of mutual dependence

458
00:37:09,700 --> 00:37:14,120
that carried economic shocks across
borders at devastating speed.

459
00:37:14,600 --> 00:37:20,200
When Wall Street collapsed in 1929, it
did not only hit the American economy.

460
00:37:20,240 --> 00:37:26,180
It destroyed the German economy, which in turn
propped up the French and British economies,

461
00:37:26,220 --> 00:37:29,780
which in turn financed the
American banking system.

462
00:37:30,100 --> 00:37:36,200
The whole thing was a closed circuit, and
when one link broke, the whole chain snapped.

463
00:37:36,720 --> 00:37:40,800
That raises perhaps the most
uncomfortable question of all:

464
00:37:40,840 --> 00:37:44,280
who was really responsible
for the Second World War?

465
00:37:44,620 --> 00:37:47,500
The simple answer is Adolf Hitler.

466
00:37:47,740 --> 00:37:51,880
And without Hitler's fanaticism the war would
certainly have taken a very different form,

467
00:37:51,920 --> 00:37:55,220
or might never have happened at all.

468
00:37:55,580 --> 00:37:58,340
But Hitler did not act in a vacuum.

469
00:37:58,800 --> 00:38:01,180
He came to power under
very specific conditions,

470
00:38:01,220 --> 00:38:07,020
and those conditions were created by
specific decisions of specific people,

471
00:38:07,060 --> 00:38:10,060
many of whom never
intended the consequences.

472
00:38:10,400 --> 00:38:13,940
The architects of the Treaty of
Versailles created the resentment.

473
00:38:14,200 --> 00:38:17,900
The bankers behind the Dawes
Plan created the dependence.

474
00:38:18,220 --> 00:38:21,960
The politicians who failed to
confront aggression in Manchuria,

475
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:28,200
Ethiopia and the Rhineland created the
expectation that aggression goes unpunished.

476
00:38:28,300 --> 00:38:33,800
And the statesmen who signed the Munich Agreement
created the final illusion that war could

477
00:38:33,840 --> 00:38:37,660
be avoided by sacrificing
the rights of smaller states.

478
00:38:38,280 --> 00:38:41,480
The Second World War was
not a natural disaster.

479
00:38:41,580 --> 00:38:51,040
It was a man-made catastrophe, assembled over decades from
vengefulness, greed, cowardice and deliberate looking away.

480
00:38:51,640 --> 00:38:57,060
Its true origins lie not in
the invasion of Poland in 1939,

481
00:38:57,100 --> 00:39:03,480
but in a chain of failures that began
the moment the guns fell silent in 1918.

482
00:39:04,100 --> 00:39:08,800
So the next time someone tells you
the Second World War began on 1

483
00:39:08,840 --> 00:39:12,720
September 1939, you will
know the whole story.

484
00:39:13,140 --> 00:39:15,320
It began twenty years earlier.

485
00:39:15,880 --> 00:39:18,840
It began with a treaty that
was meant to secure peace and

486
00:39:18,880 --> 00:39:21,600
instead prepared the ground for war.

487
00:39:21,900 --> 00:39:26,780
It began with bankers who cared
more about profits than stability.

488
00:39:27,060 --> 00:39:31,820
It began with politicians who
put appeasement above principle.

489
00:39:32,100 --> 00:39:36,600
And it began with a world that
looked away again and again,

490
00:39:36,640 --> 00:39:39,660
until it was too late
to look away any longer.

491
00:39:40,160 --> 00:39:44,160
History does not repeat
itself, but it rhymes.

492
00:39:44,740 --> 00:39:55,340
Economic crises, rising nationalism, weakened international
institutions and rulers who test the limits and meet no resistance:

493
00:39:55,380 --> 00:39:58,520
this pattern has appeared again
and again throughout history.

494
00:39:59,100 --> 00:40:05,540
Recognising it may be the most important lesson
of the most destructive war in human history.

495
00:40:05,860 --> 00:40:10,800
If this story made you think,
subscribe to Chronik der Macht.

496
00:40:11,060 --> 00:40:13,220
It helps the channel more than you think.

497
00:40:13,600 --> 00:40:15,560
See you in the next video.
